---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";

export const frontmatter = {
  title: "Building custom visuals with Figma and AI agents using the lipstick workflow",
  description: "A fast workflow for developing custom data visualizations by combining Figma design with AI coding agents.",
  pubDate: "2026-05-09",
  tags: ["Data Visualization", "AI", "Figma", "D3.js", "Looker Studio"],
};

const currentLang = "en";
const alternateHref = "/blog/custom-visuals-workflow-figma-ai/";

const lipstickVideo = "/assets/imgs/Projects/lookerstudio_lipstick_viz_optimized.mp4";
---

<MainLayout
  title="Building custom visuals with Figma and AI agents | Alexis Gomel"
  description="A fast workflow for developing custom data visualizations by combining Figma design with AI coding agents."
  currentLang={currentLang}
  alternateHref={alternateHref}
>
  <style is:inline>
    .blog-wrap {
      max-width: 760px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 120px 24px 80px;
      line-height: 1.8;
      font-size: 1.05rem;
      color: #333;
    }
    .blog-wrap h1 {
      font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 4vw, 2.2rem);
      font-weight: 700;
      line-height: 1.25;
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }
    .blog-meta {
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      color: #888;
      margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    }
    .blog-wrap h2 {
      font-size: 1.35rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      margin-top: 2.5rem;
      margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
      border-bottom: 2px solid #f0f0f0;
      padding-bottom: 0.3rem;
    }
    .blog-wrap p {
      margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    }
    .blog-video {
      width: 100%;
      border-radius: 8px;
      border: 1px solid #e5e5e5;
      box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.07);
      margin: 1rem 0 0.4rem;
    }
    .img-caption {
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      color: #999;
      text-align: center;
      margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }
    .tag {
      display: inline-block;
      background: #f4f4f4;
      border-radius: 4px;
      padding: 2px 10px;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: #555;
      margin-right: 6px;
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }
    .cta-box {
      background: #f8f8f8;
      border-left: 4px solid var(--primary, #3498db);
      padding: 24px;
      border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
      margin: 2.5rem 0;
      text-align: center;
    }
    .cta-button {
      display: inline-block;
      background: #3498db;
      color: white;
      padding: 12px 24px;
      border-radius: 6px;
      text-decoration: none;
      font-weight: 600;
      margin-top: 1rem;
      transition: background 0.2s;
    }
    .cta-button:hover {
      background: #2980b9;
      color: white;
    }
  </style>

  <article class="blog-wrap">
    <span class="tag">Data Visualization</span>
    <span class="tag">AI</span>
    <span class="tag">Figma</span>
    <span class="tag">D3.js</span>

    <h1>Building custom visuals with Figma and AI agents using the lipstick workflow</h1>
    
    <p class="blog-meta">
      Alexis Gomel · 9 May 2026
    </p>

    <video autoplay muted loop playsinline class="blog-video">
      <source src={lipstickVideo} type="video/mp4" />
      Your browser does not support the video tag.
    </video>
    <p class="img-caption">The Lipstick viz in action. A custom Looker Studio component built using this method.</p>

    <p>
      Most data visualization tools force a hard choice. You either use a generic bar chart or you spend days wrestling with D3.js boilerplates. I prefer a middle path. I call it the Lipstick workflow. It uses Figma for the soul of the visual and AI agents for the plumbing. This method keeps the creative process fast while providing absolute control over the final output.
    </p>

    <h2>The Figma foundation</h2>
    <p>
      Everything starts in Figma. I don't think about data schemas yet. I focus on the story. I draw the visual as a raw SVG. This is where I define the shapes, colors, and hover behaviors. For the Lipstick viz, this meant drawing the specific "lipstick" bar and the target indicators. Once the design is ready, I export it as an SVG.
    </p>

    <h2>Moving to the IDE</h2>
    <p>
      I take that SVG into VS Code or Antigravity. This is where I wake up the Gemini CLI. The static design is ready to become a live component. Using a CLI agent directly in the workspace makes the transition from design to code feel immediate.
    </p>

    <h2>Synthetic data and D3.js</h2>
    <p>
      The first prompt is simple. I ask the agent to create a JSON file with synthetic data that fits the visual logic. Then, I give it the Figma SVG and tell it to write D3.js code that maps the data to the SVG elements.
    </p>
    <p>
      Using the SVG as a template is the secret. It ensures the final chart looks exactly like the design. D3 handles the math and the data binding, but Figma provides the structure. This avoids the "empty canvas" problem that usually slows down custom development.
    </p>

    <h2>Refining interactions</h2>
    <p>
      Now I iterate. We add hover effects and smooth transitions. I test the code in a local browser using the synthetic data. The agent helps me fix edge cases in the rendering logic or adjust the responsive behavior. We keep polishing until the visual feels responsive and "alive."
    </p>

    <h2>Deployment</h2>
    <p>
      Finally, I ask the agent to port the working logic. Sometimes it becomes a Looker Studio community visual. Other times, it's a Power BI component or React code for a website. The hard part is already done. The transformation to a specific platform is just a wrapper change.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-box">
      <p style="font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Want to see the final results?</p>
      <p>Explore the full gallery of custom visuals I've built using this workflow, including the Lipstick chart, Tree Hierarchy, and more.</p>
      <a href="/projects/looker-studio-custom-visuals/" class="cta-button">
        Explore Custom Visuals &rarr;
      </a>
    </div>

    <p>
      This workflow lets me stay agile. I can focus on the storytelling and the aesthetics from the start. The AI acts as a technical partner that bridges the gap between a design vision and production-ready code.
    </p>
  </article>
</MainLayout>
